.. _NrSlTraces:

.. _traceUePositions:

TraceUePositions
================

The class `NrSlTraceHelper` provides several helper methods to enable traces
and plots relevant to sidelink operation.  A basic trace and plotting script
to visualize UE mobility in a sidelink simulation is available with the
method `NrSlTraceHelper::TraceUePositions()` and the corresponding Bash
script `plot-ue-positions.sh` which can be found in the `examples/sl-examples`
directory.  The Bash script requires Gnuplot to be installed on the host.

The trace file can be generated by two statements such as the below. 

.. sourcecode:: cpp

  // UE positions tracing outputs NrSlUePositionTrace.txt
  NrSlTraceHelper nrSlTraceHelper;
  nrSlTraceHelper.TraceUePositions(relayUeNodes); 

Optional arguments to `TraceUePositions` include the option to configure the
polling interval and to change the trace filename. 

The trace file can be converted to a PDF file by using the provided Bash script,
such as follows.

.. sourcecode:: shell

  $ contrib/nr/examples/sl-examples/plot-ue-positions.sh NrSlUePositionTrace.txt

The above will yield a `ue-positions.pdf` file such as the example below (illustrating motion of
two relay nodes for 15 seconds of simulation time, in this case).

#.. _fig-ue-positions:
#
#.. figure:: figures/ue-positions.*
#   :align: center
#   :scale: 70 %

.. _traceSensingAlgorithm:

TraceSensingAlgorithm
=====================

The class `NrSlTraceHelper` provides several helper methods to enable traces
and plots relevant to sidelink operation.  A trace source and plotting script
to visualize the output of the sensing algorithm is available with the
method `NrSlTraceHelper::TraceSensingAlgorithm()` and the corresponding 
Python matplotlib program `plot-sl-sensing.py` which can be found in the
`examples/sl-examples` directory.

The trace file can be generated by a few statements such as the below. 

.. sourcecode:: cpp

    NrSlTraceHelper nrSlTraceHelper;
    auto ueDevice0 = ueNetDev.Get(0)->GetObject<NrUeNetDevice>();
    auto ueMac0 = ueDevice0->GetMac(0)->GetObject<NrSlUeMac>();
    nrSlTraceHelper.TraceSensingAlgorithm(ueMac0, "sl-basic-example-sensing.csv");

In the above, the method requires a pointer to the NrSlUeMac instance that
is being traced (note that there can be more than one such instance on
a sidelink device), and a trace file name.  The resulting trace file
will contain an entry for each time that the sensing algorithm is executed,
with an annotation displaying metadata about the output data, such as:

.. sourcecode:: text

    #Trace,time=2.02,slot=8080,node=1,len=432,subch=5,LsubCH=1,t0=400,t1=2,t2=33

followed by an array of values that spans the sensing and selection window in
time and the number of subchannels in frequency.  The values correspond
to color choices that will be plotted by the plotting program.

The csv file can contain a lot of data and is not intended to be human-readable.
The plotting program can be run on the file to display what IMSI values and
slot IDs are available for plotting, such as:

.. sourcecode:: text

    $ python3 plot-sl-sensing.py --file=sl-basic-example-sensing.csv --list
    Slots available to print: [8080, 8160, 8240, ... 16160]
    Nodes available to print: [1] 

The slot and node combination can then be selected to yield a plot such
as follows:

.. sourcecode:: text

    $ python3 plot-sl-sensing.py --file=sl-basic-example-sensing.csv --node=1 --slot=16160

#.. _fig-sensing:
#
#.. figure:: figures/sl-basic-example-sensing-16160-1.png
#   :align: center
#   :scale: 70 %

The above figure shows two subplots.  The top subplot illustrates the sensing
window and shows with dark lines (spanning all subchannels) the slots that
are part of the node's transmit history, for which no sensing data is available,
and orange lines (with hue varying according to RSRP strength) corresponding
to sensed SCI-1 slots.  The bottom subplot illustrates the selection window
with dark colors indicating the candidate resources provided to the scheduler.
Resources that are not dark in this window correspond either to non-sidelink
slots or to resources that are excluded by sensing.

.. _traceSchedulingAlgorithm:

TraceSchedulingAlgorithm
========================

Similar to the previous trace, a trace source and plotting script
to visualize the output of the scheduling algorithm is available with the
method `NrSlTraceHelper::TraceSchedulingAlgorithm()` and the corresponding 
Python matplotlib program `plot-sl-scheduling.py` which can be found in the
`examples/sl-examples` directory.

The trace file can be generated by a few statements such as the below. 

.. sourcecode:: cpp

    NrSlTraceHelper nrSlTraceHelper;
    auto ueDevice0 = ueNetDev.Get(0)->GetObject<NrUeNetDevice>();
    auto ueMac0 = ueDevice0->GetMac(0)->GetObject<NrSlUeMac>();
    nrSlTraceHelper.TraceSchedulingAlgorithm(ueMac0, "sl-basic-example-scheduling.csv");

In the above, the method requires a pointer to the NrSlUeMac instance that
is being traced (note that there can be more than one such instance on
a sidelink device), and a trace file name.  The resulting trace file
will contain an entry for each time that the scheduling algorithm is executed,
with an annotation displaying metadata about the output data, such as:

.. sourcecode:: text

    #Trace,time=2.02,slot=8080,node=1,len=34,subch=5,lSubCh=1,...

followed by an array of values that spans the sselection window in
time and the number of subchannels in frequency.  The values correspond
to color choices that will be plotted by the plotting program.

The csv file can contain a lot of data and is not intended to be human-readable.
The plotting program can be run on the file to display what IMSI values and
slot IDs are available for plotting, such as:

.. sourcecode:: text
    
    $ python3 plot-sl-scheduling.py --file=sl-basic-example-scheduling.csv --list
    Slots available to print: [8080, 8160, 8240, ... 16160]
    Nodes available to print: [1]

The slot and node combination can then be selected to yield a plot such
as follows:

.. sourcecode:: text

    $ python3 plot-sl-scheduling.py --file=sl-basic-example-scheduling.csv --node=1 --slot=16160

#.. _fig-scheduling:
#
#.. figure:: figures/sl-basic-example-scheduling-16160-1.png
#   :align: center
#   :scale: 70 %

The above figure illustrates the selection window only; although an SPS
grant's reserved resources may span many slots after the initial selection
window, the plot becomes too compressed horizontally to read if all resources
are shown.  The dark blue resources indicate the initial candidates to the
algorithm, and the light blue show existing grants, if any.  The red slots
show the new grant; if retransmissions are selected more than one red resource
will be shown.  The subtitle provides more information on the parameters
that were used in the scheduling.
